RDP load balancing + high availability for the ldm xfreerdp backend
=====================================================================

Background: for a Linux-based xrdp server pool (no Windows RD Connection
Broker available), the standard approach is HAProxy in TCP mode in front
of the xrdp servers. This document covers three things people usually
need together: load balancing across xrdp servers, high availability of
the load balancer itself, and a staging environment for validating
updates on a live pilot group before a wider rollout (a NIS2/KRITIS-style
requirement for critical infrastructure).

The example config files referenced below are in this same directory:
haproxy-rdp.cfg, keepalived-lb1.conf, keepalived-lb2.conf.


Why HAProxy TCP mode, and why a stick-table (not plain "balance source")
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

HAProxy in "mode tcp" forwards bytes without parsing the RDP protocol at
all - it doesn't understand or need to understand the RDP handshake,
TLS negotiation, or the username exchanged inside it. That also means it
CANNOT route based on the RDP username: that's exchanged encrypted,
invisible to a plain TCP proxy. Anything routing on "which user is this"
would need an RDP-aware proxy, which doesn't exist for xrdp - not
something worth building from scratch.

What HAProxy CAN see is the client's source IP, which is what per-client
stickiness is built on. This matters specifically for RDP: sessions are
stateful, so a client that disconnects and reconnects (an auto-reconnect,
a network blip) needs to land back on the SAME backend server to find
its existing session - "balance leastconn" or "roundrobin" alone would
happily send the reconnect to a different server, orphaning the old
session. LTSP thin clients with static/DHCP-reserved IPs are exactly the
case source-IP stickiness works well for. (Caveat: if clients instead sat
behind a shared NAT gateway, they'd all map to the same backend - not a
concern for LTSP thin clients on their own LAN addresses, but worth
knowing if the network layout ever changes.)

Use a stick-table for this, not plain "balance source" (IP-hash) -
haproxy-rdp.cfg does this:

    balance leastconn
    stick-table type ip size 200k expire 12h
    stick on src

The difference matters in exactly the failure case this whole document
is about. Plain IP-hash recomputes a client's target from the CURRENT
list of live servers on every single connection - so when a client's
server dies and it fails over to another one, the hash silently sends it
BACK to the original server the moment that server recovers, tearing
down a perfectly healthy, currently-running RDP session for no reason
other than "the original server came back". A stick-table entry, once
created, is only ever replaced when it expires (here: 12h of client
inactivity) or the server it currently points to itself becomes
unavailable - a recovered server does not reclaim clients that already
failed over elsewhere. That's the actual requirement: a client's session
should keep living whichever server it's already on until the user logs
out, or that server dies too - never just because its original server is
healthy again.

Verified live (see the ltsp6/ldm-mabutz development history for the full
walkthrough): per-client stickiness, automatic failover when a backend
goes down, a real xrdp server reachable end-to-end through the proxy
with a full RDP/TLS handshake, and - specifically re-tested after an
initial plain-IP-hash version showed the failback problem above - that a
stick-table-based backend correctly keeps a failed-over client on its
new server even after its original server comes back, only moving it
again if that new server itself later fails.


Staging: a defined client group tests updates before everyone else
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Since HAProxy can't distinguish RDP users, staging routing has to happen
before the RDP layer even starts - i.e. based on which physical client
connects where. Two ways to do that; the recommended one uses ldm's own
existing per-client configuration instead of adding a second place to
maintain a client list:

  A) HAProxy ACL on client source IP:
       acl is_staging_client src 10.0.0.50 10.0.0.51 10.0.0.52
       use_backend rdp_staging if is_staging_client
       default_backend rdp_production
     One shared backend pool config, an ACL list to maintain in HAProxy.

  B) Per-client lts.conf (recommended): the pilot group's thin clients
     get RDP_SERVER=<VIP_STAGING> in their lts.conf (by MAC or hostname,
     same mechanism you already use for other per-client settings),
     everyone else gets RDP_SERVER=<VIP_PROD>. Two entirely separate
     HAProxy frontends/backends (see haproxy-rdp.cfg: rdp_prod_in /
     rdp_staging_in), no shared ACL list to keep in sync elsewhere.
     Cleaner for compliance documentation too: staging vs. production is
     a clear, separately-configured environment, not a routing rule
     buried in the load balancer.

haproxy-rdp.cfg implements B: two frontends (VIP_PROD, VIP_STAGING), each
with its own backend pool. Roll an update out to the staging backend
pool first, validate with the pilot group, then apply it to the
production pool.

Verified live, using haproxy-rdp.cfg's exact frontend/backend structure
(two VIPs, two independent backend pools):

  - Routing isolation: repeated connections to VIP_PROD only ever reached
    the production pool, repeated connections to VIP_STAGING only ever
    reached the staging pool - never crossed over, in either direction.
  - The actual point of having a separate staging pool: killing the
    staging backend(s) left the production pool's health checks and
    connections completely unaffected (still all UP, still routing
    normally) - and, checked the other way round, killing all production
    backends left the staging pool completely unaffected too. A bad
    staging rollout - or, just as usefully, a production outage - cannot
    cross the boundary between the two environments; each is its own
    failure domain as far as HAProxy is concerned.

To repeat this validation yourselves after any config change: watch the
stats page (port 8404) while stopping xrdp on a staging server - only
the "rdp_staging" backend should show servers going DOWN, "rdp_production"
must stay all-UP throughout, and vice versa.


High availability of the load balancer itself
-------------------------------------------------

HAProxy is otherwise a single point of failure: if its host dies, every
xrdp server behind it becomes unreachable regardless of how healthy they
are. Two complementary mechanisms, worth combining rather than choosing
one:

  - keepalived + VRRP: two VMs (lb1, lb2), each running the identical
    haproxy-rdp.cfg plus keepalived. keepalived moves a floating virtual
    IP (actually two here - VIP_PROD and VIP_STAGING, moved together)
    between the two nodes. Failover time: well under a second, because
    the second HAProxy instance is already running and just starts
    responding on the VIP.

  - Proxmox HA: put both lb1 and lb2 VMs under Proxmox's own HA manager,
    on an anti-affinity rule so they never land on the same physical
    node. This protects against a Proxmox node itself dying - but the
    failover time is much longer (Proxmox has to detect the node is gone,
    fence it, then boot the VM elsewhere: commonly 30s-2min), during
    which any RDP sessions on that node are hard-disconnected.

Combining both: keepalived's VRRP failover handles "haproxy died but the
VM/host is fine" (sub-second), Proxmox HA's node-level failover handles
"the whole physical host died" (slower, but keepalived's fast path
doesn't apply there anyway since the VM itself is gone). Proxmox HA
alone, without keepalived, is a reasonable simplification if the longer
failover gap during an actual host failure is acceptable.

keepalived config notes (see keepalived-lb1.conf/keepalived-lb2.conf for
the full, commented versions) - three real keepalived 2.x gotchas that
are easy to get wrong and fail silently or behave unexpectedly:

  - A vrrp_script's "weight" is ADDED to priority on success, not
    subtracted on failure - for a "reduce priority when unhealthy" check
    (which is what you want here), it must be a NEGATIVE number, and
    large enough to bring the master's priority below the backup's
    priority (e.g. master 150, backup 100, weight -60 -> 90 < 100).
  - The config directive is "enable_script_security", not
    "script_security" (an easy typo that keepalived accepts as an
    "unknown keyword" without failing the whole config load).
  - Add "user root" to the vrrp_script block - keepalived's default
    script user (keepalived_script) doesn't exist on a plain Debian
    install, so the script's real exit status is masked as a "SECURITY
    VIOLATION" unless a valid user is set explicitly.


Proxmox HA setup for lb1/lb2
---------------------------------

Verified live on a real (nested, 2-node) Proxmox VE 9.2 cluster - see
"Live test results" below for what was actually observed, including two
gotchas not covered by older documentation.

Goal: lb1 and lb2 normally run on two different Proxmox nodes, and
Proxmox's own HA manager restarts either one elsewhere if its node dies
(on top of, not instead of, the keepalived VRRP failover above - see the
"combining both" note above for why you want both).

1. Create the two VMs (e.g. VMIDs 200 and 201) and install them as
   described elsewhere in this document, one on each of two physical
   nodes - say "pve-a" and "pve-b".

2. As of Proxmox VE 9.0, classic HA groups (`ha-manager groupadd`) are
   deprecated in favor of HA rules; `groupadd` now fails outright with
   "ha groups have been migrated to rules". Use a non-strict node
   affinity rule per VM instead:

     ha-manager add vm:200 --state started
     ha-manager add vm:201 --state started
     ha-manager rules add node-affinity ha-rule-lb1 --resources vm:200 --nodes pve-a
     ha-manager rules add node-affinity ha-rule-lb2 --resources vm:201 --nodes pve-b

   Deliberately leave out `--strict 1`: a strict node-affinity rule
   refuses to run the resource anywhere but the named node, which is the
   opposite of what you want for actual host-failure recovery. Non-strict
   (the default) means "prefer this node, but relocate elsewhere if it's
   unavailable" - Proxmox's `ha-manager groups` documentation still shows
   the old `groupadd` syntax; if your `ha-manager add` rejects it with
   the error above, your PVE version needs the `rules add` form instead.

3. For true anti-affinity (never co-locate lb1 and lb2, even during a
   node failure), add a negative resource-affinity rule instead of/in
   addition to the node-affinity rules above:

     ha-manager rules add resource-affinity keep-separate --affinity negative --resources vm:200,vm:201

4. Critical: **lb1 and lb2's virtual disks must be on shared or
   replicated storage reachable from both nodes** - not each node's local
   storage. This was the one thing that didn't work in live testing (see
   below): ha-manager detected the dead node and correctly retried
   starting the VM elsewhere every ~10s, but failed every time with
   "volume '...' does not exist", because the disk image physically only
   existed on the dead node's local disk. Use shared storage (NFS/iSCSI/
   Ceph) or ZFS storage replication (`pvesr`) between the specific node
   pair for this to actually work, not just detect the failure.

5. Test it: while both VMs are healthy and on separate nodes, put the
   node currently running lb1 (the active VRRP master) into maintenance
   mode (or just power it off in a lab/test setting) and confirm both
   that Proxmox restarts lb1's VM on the other available node, and that
   keepalived on lb2 has already taken over the VIP in the meantime (the
   VRRP failover should happen in well under the time Proxmox needs to
   even detect the node is gone).


Live test results
---------------------
Tested on a real 2-node Proxmox VE 9.2.11 cluster (nested inside Hyper-V,
but the cluster/HA software stack itself behaves identically to bare
metal) with lb1/lb2 as qemu VMs running haproxy + keepalived. Hard-killed
the node running lb1 (Stop-VM equivalent to pulling power) and observed:

  - Corosync/pmxcfs correctly detected the dead node and the surviving
    node kept/regained quorum (see the `two_node` gotcha below).
  - ha-manager's CRM correctly identified the dead node's lrm as
    "old timestamp - dead?", fenced it (watchdog-based fencing, armed by
    default), and began relocating vm:200 to the surviving node - all
    automatic, no manual intervention needed to *detect and attempt*
    the failover.
  - The relocation itself then failed repeatedly (every ~10s, per
    ha-manager's restart policy) with "volume '...' does not exist" -
    exactly the local-storage gotcha in step 4 above. The failure mode
    itself is instructive: ha-manager doesn't silently give up or corrupt
    anything, it just keeps retrying forever until either the resource
    is fixed (shared storage) or manually stopped.
  - keepalived on lb2 (the surviving node's VM) correctly took over the
    VIP while lb1 was down, and failed back automatically once lb1 came
    back up and haproxy was healthy there again - independent of whatever
    ha-manager was doing with the VM placement.
  - Bringing the dead node back online surfaced a second real gotcha:
    corosync refused to start with "Received config version (N) is
    different than my config version (N-1)! Exiting" - the node's local
    corosync.conf was stale from before it went down, and pmxcfs (backing
    /etc/pve) is read-only on a node without quorum, so the stale config
    can't just be edited in place. Recovery: `pmxcfs -l` starts pmxcfs in
    local (non-clustered) mode, which allows writing the correct
    corosync.conf even without quorum; then kill that local pmxcfs
    instance and start the normal `pve-cluster`/`corosync` services.
  - Related: `two_node: 1` in corosync.conf (needed so a lone surviving
    node keeps quorum at all - see the next section) implicitly enables
    `wait_for_all` unless you explicitly set `wait_for_all: 0`. That
    matters because `wait_for_all` blocks quorum from being regranted
    after any corosync ring reset until *every* configured node has
    rejoined at least once - which will never happen if one of them is
    genuinely, permanently gone. Symptom: `pvecm status` reports "No
    quorum" and "Quorum: 1 Activity blocked" on the lone survivor even
    though it should have quorum by itself. Fix (once quorum is
    otherwise available to make /etc/pve writable again):
    `pvecm expected 1` forces quorum immediately as a manual override;
    for the long-term fix, explicitly set `wait_for_all: 0` in
    corosync.conf.


Deployment checklist
-------------------------

1. Two small VMs (lb1, lb2) on different Proxmox nodes, each also
   covered by Proxmox HA (see "Proxmox HA setup for lb1/lb2" above).
2. Install haproxy and keepalived on both.
3. Deploy haproxy-rdp.cfg (identical on both nodes) - fill in the real
   VIP_PROD/VIP_STAGING addresses and backend server IPs.
4. Deploy keepalived-lb1.conf on lb1, keepalived-lb2.conf on lb2 - fill
   in the real interface name, unicast_src_ip/unicast_peer addresses,
   and match the virtual_ipaddress block to the same VIPs used in step 3.
5. In lts.conf: RDP_SERVER=<VIP_PROD> by default; the pilot/staging
   client group gets RDP_SERVER=<VIP_STAGING> instead (by MAC/hostname).
6. Validate: pull a backend out of the pool (stop xrdp on it) and
   confirm HAProxy's stats page (port 8404) marks it down and traffic
   keeps flowing via the others; then stop haproxy on whichever node
   currently holds the VIPs and confirm the other node picks them up
   within a second or two (`ip addr show` on the VM's NIC).
